WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.002
Since 1985, a Costco hot dog and soda has cost $1.50.

2
00:00:05.282 --> 00:00:08.774
Gas went up. Rent went up. Maybe even your allowance went up.

3
00:00:09.054 --> 00:00:11.125
The hot dog? Still $1.50.

4
00:00:12.075 --> 00:00:16.114
Costco earns very little on every combo, and it may even lose money.

5
00:00:16.394 --> 00:00:17.976
So why keep the price frozen?

6
00:00:18.926 --> 00:00:22.185
Because the hot dog isn't really the product. It's the hook.

7
00:00:22.465 --> 00:00:26.690
A super-cheap item that pulls shoppers into the store is called a loss leader.

8
00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.986
Once you're inside, you grab a giant tub of cashews,

9
00:00:29.986 --> 00:00:32.596
a sixty-pack of batteries, and maybe a kayak.

10
00:00:33.546 --> 00:00:36.440
And because the price never changes, it's also a

11
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.878
customary price, one that people expect to stay the same.

12
00:00:40.158 --> 00:00:42.426
Raising it would feel like breaking a promise.

13
00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:46.274
The real profit comes from everything else members buy,

14
00:00:46.274 --> 00:00:48.751
and from renewing their memberships every year.

15
00:00:49.031 --> 00:00:52.726
Your turn. What other store uses a loss leader to get you in the door?
